<article class="env-lemma" id="0BJS">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BJS">Lemma <span data-tag="0BJS">51.11.2</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a finite homomorphism of Noetherian rings. Let $I \subset A$ be an ideal and set $J = IB$. Let $M$ be a finite $B$-module. If $A$ is universally catenary, then $s_{B, J}(M) = s_{A, I}(M)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\mathfrak p \subset \mathfrak q \subset A$ be primes with $I \subset \mathfrak q$ and $I \not\subset \mathfrak p$. Since $A \to B$ is finite there are finitely many primes $\mathfrak p_ i$ lying over $\mathfrak p$. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AUK" data-tag="0AUK">10.72.11</a> we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{depth}(M_\mathfrak p) = \min \text{depth}(M_{\mathfrak p_ i})  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Let $\mathfrak p_ i \subset \mathfrak q_{ij}$ be primes lying over $\mathfrak q$. By going up for $A \to B$ (Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00GU" data-tag="00GU">10.36.22</a>) there is at least one $\mathfrak q_{ij}$ for each $i$. Then we see that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \dim ((B/\mathfrak p_ i)_{\mathfrak q_{ij}}) = \dim ((A/\mathfrak p)_\mathfrak q)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       by the dimension formula, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/02IJ" data-tag="02IJ">10.113.1</a>. This implies that the minimum of the quantities used to define $s_{B, J}(M)$ for the pairs $(\mathfrak p_ i, \mathfrak q_{ij})$ is equal to the quantity for the pair $(\mathfrak p, \mathfrak q)$. This proves the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>